{"alignments":[],"candidates":[{"additionalConceptCount":0,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 12","reason":"Write z=exp(2πik/24), where k=0,...,23. Then z^6=i^k, which is real exactly when k is even. There are 12 such indices.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_12A_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"real-power-reduction","label":"Reduce to twelfth roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"real-power-reduction":["math:roots-of-unity"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"factor-sixth-powers","label":"Factor by the sixth power","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-sixth-powers":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_152"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_152"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 2004","reason":"Set z=a+bi. The solution z=0 works. For z≠0, taking moduli gives |z|^2002=|z|, hence |z|=1 and conjugate(z)=1/z. The equation becomes z^2003=1, which has 2003 distinct roots. Including zero gives 2004 pairs.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2002_AMC_12A_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_h098"],"id":"polar-argument-count","label":"Compare magnitudes and arguments","methodDirectNodeIds":{"polar-argument-count":["math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-polar-de-moivre"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h097","cnm_h098"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 2^2005","reason":"All terms have modulus 1, so conjugate(z_n)=1/z_n and z_(n+1)=iz_n². Iteration gives z_N=i^(2^N-1)z_0^(2^N). At N=2005 this is -iz_0^(2^2005), so z_0^(2^2005)=i. This equation has exactly 2^2005 distinct roots, all of modulus 1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2005_AMC_12B_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"id":"backward-square-roots","label":"Count two preimages at every step","nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h231"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_h103","cnm_h102"],"id":"solution:backward-square-roots:27794b62f4d8","label":"Count predecessors backward","methodDirectNodeIds":{"backward-square-roots":["math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h231"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h231","cnm_h098","cnm_h103","cnm_h102"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_h103","cnm_h102"],"id":"iterate-squaring","label":"Turn the recurrence into repeated squaring","methodDirectNodeIds":{"iterate-squaring":["math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h200"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","cnm_h200","cnm_h098","cnm_h103","cnm_h102"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 2","reason":"Writing the bases as z and w, direct multiplication gives z²=w and zw=1, so z³=w³=1. Because 2022=3·674, both terms equal 1 and f(2022)=2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12B_Q11","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"algebraic-cube-cycle","label":"Verify the cube cycle algebraically","methodDirectNodeIds":{"algebraic-cube-cycle":["cnm_h102","math:roots-of-unity"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h102","math:roots-of-unity"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"polar-angle-multiplication","label":"Polar form and De Moivre","methodDirectNodeIds":{"polar-angle-multiplication":["math:complex-polar-de-moivre","math:roots-of-unity"]},"nodeIds":["math:complex-polar-de-moivre","math:roots-of-unity"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E, 7","reason":"Taking moduli gives |z|^5=|z|, so z=0 or |z|=1. In the latter case, conjugate(z)=1/z, giving z^6=1. Its six distinct roots all satisfy the original equation, for seven solutions total.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2023_AMC_12A_Q14","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"modulus-and-algebraic-roots","label":"Compare moduli, then factor","methodDirectNodeIds":{"modulus-and-algebraic-roots":["cnm_h098","cnm_h104","math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-quadratic-solutions"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h098","cnm_h104","math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-quadratic-solutions"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"polar-angle-count","label":"Compare lengths and angles","methodDirectNodeIds":{"polar-angle-count":["cnm_h098","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","math:roots-of-unity"]},"nodeIds":["cnm_h098","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","math:roots-of-unity"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D, 4","reason":"The roots have modulus 2 and arguments 30,90,150,210,270,330 degrees. Only square root of 3 plus i and square root of 3 minus i have positive real part; their product is 3 + 1 = 4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1990_AMC_12_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_146"],"id":"factor-into-quadratics","label":"Factor into simple quadratic equations","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-into-quadratics":["math:roots-of-unity","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_152","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_152","math:completing-square-expression","cnm_h102","cnm_h097","cnm_146"]},{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_146","cnm_076"],"id":"real-part-of-cube","label":"Use the real part of the cube","methodDirectNodeIds":{"real-part-of-cube":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h099","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h099","cnm_h102","cnm_h097","cnm_146","cnm_076"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B: 8√2","reason":"P(z)=(z^4-1)(z^4+7+4√3). Its roots are (±1,0), (0,±1), and (±a,±a), where a=(1+√3)/2. The minimum pairwise distance is √2, with a²+(a-1)²=2. Ordering the roots by angle produces eight edges of that length, attaining 8√2.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2011_AMC_12B_Q24","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_h098","cnm_h102","cnm_173"],"id":"solution:cartesian-distance-bound:d495bc8865f8","label":"Locate the roots and bound every edge","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cartesian-distance-bound":["math:roots-of-unity","math:extremal-principle","cnm_h173","cnm_152"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:extremal-principle","cnm_h173","cnm_152","cnm_h097","cnm_h098","cnm_h102","cnm_173"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B (92)","reason":"Set (u1,u2,u3,u4,u5)=(4-a,a-b,b-c,c-d,d), with sum 4. Since P(1)>0, a unit zero z is not 1. From (z-1)P(z)=0, obtain 4=sum uj·z^(-j). Equality in the unit-circle bound forces z^j=1 whenever uj>0. Thus positive weights are supported within {2,4}, {3}, or {5}. These yield P(1) values 8,10,12,14,16, plus 12 and 20. Their sum is 92.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2012_AMC_12B_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_146","cnm_h098","cnm_h100","cnm_029"],"id":"coefficient-differences-and-real-parts","label":"Turn ordered coefficients into nonnegative differences","methodDirectNodeIds":{"coefficient-differences-and-real-parts":["math:roots-of-unity","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:nonnegative-sum-zero"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:integer-diophantine-equations","math:nonnegative-sum-zero","cnm_146","cnm_h098","cnm_h100","cnm_029"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":2,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, 36","reason":"Here z²=i, z⁴=-1, and z⁸=1. Among k=1,...,12, six odd k have k² congruent to 1 modulo 8, while three even k give residue 4 and three give residue 0. Thus the first sum is 6z-3+3=6z, and the reciprocal sum is 6/z. Their product is 36.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2019_AMC_12A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"square-exponents-modulo-eight","label":"Reduce the exponents modulo 8","methodDirectNodeIds":{"square-exponents-modulo-eight":["math:roots-of-unity","math:modular-congruence","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:modular-congruence","cnm_h102"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A: 4","reason":"Since 1+w+w^2 = 0, the two factors equal -2w and -2w^2. Their product is 4w^3 = 4.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1971_AMC_12_Q22","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_142"],"id":"use-the-cubic-root-identity","label":"Use the relation among the powers of w","methodDirectNodeIds":{"use-the-cubic-root-identity":["math:roots-of-unity","math:sum-difference-cubes"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:sum-difference-cubes","cnm_h097","cnm_142"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 2√2 + √6","reason":"Every root has modulus 64^(1/12) = √2. The positive-real-part roots have arguments 0, ±π/6, and ±π/3. Their sum is √2[1 + 2cos(π/6) + 2cos(π/3)] = 2√2 + √6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2017_AMC_12B_Q12","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"polar-roots-and-pairing","label":"Locate polar roots and pair them","methodDirectNodeIds":{"polar-roots-and-pairing":["math:complex-polar-de-moivre","math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h100","cnm_277"]},"nodeIds":["math:complex-polar-de-moivre","math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h100","cnm_277"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, (3/2)(√2 − 1)","reason":"The roots are z = −6 + √3 exp(ikπ/4), so the circumradius is √3. The five unordered cyclic-gap types summing to 8 give areas (3/2)(√2 − 1), 3/2, 3√2/2, 3, and (3/2)(1 + √2). The first is smallest and is attained by three consecutive vertices.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2018_AMC_12B_Q16","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"translated-octagon-coordinates","label":"Translate the roots and use coordinates","methodDirectNodeIds":{"translated-octagon-coordinates":["math:roots-of-unity","math:extremal-principle","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_084"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:extremal-principle","math:triangle-area-radius-relations","cnm_084"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 2","reason":"The nonzero constant term excludes zero roots. Degree 5 permits only one orbit {u, uω, uω²}, with u real. The nonreal conjugate roots have a common multiplicity k, so the multiplicities are (5-2k,k,k), giving only (3,1,1) and (1,2,2). In both cases the constant term is -u^5 = 2020, which fixes u uniquely. Both multiplicity patterns produce a valid real polynomial.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2020_AMC_12B_Q17","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"root-triples-and-multiplicities","label":"Root triples and repeated roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"root-triples-and-multiplicities":["math:roots-of-unity","math:conjugate-polynomial-roots","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:fundamental-theorem-algebra"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:conjugate-polynomial-roots","math:polynomial-roots-coefficients","math:fundamental-theorem-algebra"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":4,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C, x⁹ + y⁹ = 2 rather than -1","reason":"We have x³ = y³ = 1 and x + y = x² + y² = -1. Therefore the ninth-power sum is 2, whereas every other listed exponent gives a sum of -1.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1985_AMC_12_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_h100","cnm_h040"],"id":"three-step-complex-power-cycle","label":"Find the repeating power cycle","methodDirectNodeIds":{"three-step-complex-power-cycle":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h102"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h102","cnm_h097","cnm_h100","cnm_h040"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":6,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 8 nonreal zeros","reason":"The polynomial is p(x^4), so its zeros satisfy x^4=2009, x^4=9002, or x^4=2009+9002πi. Each positive-real equation has two real and two nonreal roots; the nonreal right-hand side has four nonreal roots. All roots are distinct, giving 2+2+4=8 nonreal zeros.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2009_AMC_12A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_h102","cnm_029","cnm_032"],"id":"factor-and-count-fourth-roots","label":"Recognize the polynomial as \\(p(x^4)\\)","methodDirectNodeIds":{"factor-and-count-fourth-roots":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:roots-of-unity","math:fundamental-theorem-algebra"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:roots-of-unity","math:fundamental-theorem-algebra","cnm_h097","cnm_h102","cnm_029","cnm_032"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":7,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 2^(5/4)","reason":"The equation is (z+i)^4=1+i. The four shifted roots have modulus 2^(1/8) and consecutive arguments differing by pi/2. Their square has area 2(2^(1/8))^2=2^(5/4).","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2008_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h097","cnm_h098","cnm_072","cnm_p148","cnm_h039"],"id":"translated-fourth-power","label":"Translate the roots and use quarter-turns","methodDirectNodeIds":{"translated-fourth-power":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h102","cnm_152"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h102","cnm_152","cnm_h097","cnm_h098","cnm_072","cnm_p148","cnm_h039"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":8,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"B, (2/9)sin20°","reason":"Let S = Σ(k w^k), k = 1,...,9. Since w⁹ = 1 and Σw^k = 0, (1 - w)S = -9w. Therefore |S|⁻¹ = |1 - w|/9 = 2sin20°/9.","status":"matches"},"assessed":true,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1984_AMC_12_Q30","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":["cnm_h100","cnm_h102","cnm_h060","cnm_h062"],"id":"shift-subtract-weighted-sum","label":"Shift and subtract the weighted sum","methodDirectNodeIds":{"shift-subtract-weighted-sum":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h098","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","math:telescoping","cnm_h073"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","cnm_h098","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","math:telescoping","cnm_h073","cnm_h100","cnm_h102","cnm_h060","cnm_h062"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A, 6","reason":"(x-1)g(x)=x⁶-1, so x⁶ has remainder 1 modulo g, and therefore x¹² does too. Each of the six terms of g(x¹²) consequently has remainder 1, giving remainder 6.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1977_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"evaluate-at-roots-of-unity","label":"Evaluate at the roots of g","methodDirectNodeIds":{"evaluate-at-roots-of-unity":["math:roots-of-unity","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"C: 21","reason":"Modulo x²+x+1, x³≡1 and x+1≡-x², so the polynomial reduces to 1+x²ⁿ+x⁴ⁿ. This is 3 when 3 divides n and is 0 otherwise. Of the choices, only 21 is divisible by 3.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"1980_AMC_12_Q28","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"primitive-cube-roots","label":"Evaluate at primitive cube roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"primitive-cube-roots":["math:roots-of-unity","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"A. R(z)=-z","reason":"Modulo the polynomial z²+z+1, we have z³≡1. Since 2021=3·673+2, z^2021+1≡z²+1≡-z. This already has degree below two.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2021_AMC_12B_Q20","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"cube-root-evaluation","label":"Evaluate at the two cube roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"cube-root-evaluation":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:roots-of-unity"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:roots-of-unity"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"evaluate-at-cube-roots","label":"Evaluate at the divisor's roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"evaluate-at-cube-roots":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:roots-of-unity"]},"nodeIds":["math:polynomial-remainder-factor","math:roots-of-unity"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":1,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"E (x^6+x^3+1)","reason":"Set y=x³, so P=y^674+y^337+1. Modulo y²+y+1, we have y³=1. Since 337≡1 modulo 3, the remainder is y²+y+1=0. Therefore x⁶+x³+1 divides P.","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2022_AMC_12A_Q21","reviewStatus":"machine_scope_audited","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"roots-of-unity","label":"Check the candidate's complex roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"roots-of-unity":["math:roots-of-unity","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]},{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"ninth-roots-of-unity","label":"Interpret the complex roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"ninth-roots-of-unity":["math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:complex-polar-de-moivre","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"adjudication":"Native reconciliation under the recorded task assumptions; no blind-review claim.","derivedAnswer":"E can be nonzero","reason":"Conjugate pairing makes sum b_k=0. The given equality makes sum a_k=0 too, so the full root sum is 0 and c_2003=0. The zero root gives c_0=0. Even degree with distinct roots and conjugate nonreal pairs forces another real root, making the product of b_2 through b_2004 zero. E is possible: P(x)=x^2004-2x has simple zero root and 2003 distinct nonzero roots, satisfies the root-sum condition, and has sum c_k=P(1)=-1.","recordedAnswer":"E","status":"matches"},"assessed":false,"bank":"amc","coverage":"canonical_concept_only","flags":[],"questionId":"2004_AMC_12A_Q23","reviewStatus":"machine_adjudicated","routes":[{"contextNodeIds":[],"id":"nonzero-coefficient-witness","label":"Verify the explicit polynomial witness and its distinct roots","methodDirectNodeIds":{"nonzero-coefficient-witness":["math:roots-of-unity","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]},"nodeIds":["math:roots-of-unity","math:polynomial-remainder-factor"]}]},{"additionalConceptCount":3,"answerCheck":{"derivedAnswer":"D: 2√21","reason":"A={2,-1+i√3,-1-i√3}, while the second polynomial factors as (z-8)(z²-8), giving B={8,2√2,-2√2}. For b∈B, squared distances are (b-2)² or (b+1)²+3. Their maximum is 84, attained between 8 and either nonreal point 